#!/usr/bin/env bash
# posse-landed-check — this repo's answer to ADR 0088's one contract: is the
# tree at $PWD, which has just LANDED on main, green?
#
#   scripts/posse-landed-check          judge $PWD; exit 0 green, nonzero red
#   scripts/posse-landed-check --plan   print the commands it would run, run none
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. ranger-base-s64a5 shipped a stale-register pin and
# ranger-base-in8dr added the arms that make it fire. Each seat ran the doors
# on its own base, which held only its own half, and both runs were green. The
# replay type-checks, so ADR 0087's land check passed it, and `make test` then
# exited 2 in 31.9s for every seat on the box — found about five hours later by
# the verify lane, because nothing reads main's exit code (ci.yml fires on the
# operator's push, hours after that). This is the third case of "a gate that
# cannot reach the pin that reds" (ranger-base-rulbl gofmt;
# ranger-base-quzcd/n8wek the silent-revert audit). The first two were answered
# with prose. This one is answered with a reader.
#
# THE CONTRACT (ADR 0088 D1, which is ADR 0087 D1's verbatim), and every line
# of it is load-bearing here:
#
#   * No arguments, and **cwd is the tree to judge**. The copy that RUNS comes
#     from the last tree recorded green, not from the tree being judged — a
#     landing that weakens this script does not change what judges it. So
#     nothing here may resolve a path out of $BASH_SOURCE or $0: that names a
#     DIFFERENT tree, and a check that judged its own directory would pass
#     every landing by judging the recorded-green tree twice while looking
#     exactly like a check. Relative paths and $PWD only — which is also why
#     the derivation below reads `Makefile` and not an absolute path to one.
#   * Exit 0 is green, any other exit is red, errors on stderr.
#   * The LAST lines of combined output are the cause — the runner keeps the
#     last few non-empty ones and flattens them into a bead somebody reads
#     later (`landCheckSaid`, internal/posse/landcheck.go). A SERIAL runner
#     got that for free by failing fast and printing nothing after the dead
#     tool: make's own `*** [Makefile:NNN: corpus-check] Error 1` was the
#     tail, and it names the door, which is the whole of what the bead needs
#     to say. Under `-j` it is not free — make announces the dead door and
#     then waits for the jobs already running, so the last lines are a GREEN
#     door's output — so since D6.7 the tail is MADE rather than inherited.
#     See THE BODY RUNS FOUR DOORS AT A TIME below; this script's pin asserts
#     both halves over planted Makefiles.
#
# WHAT IT MAY RUN, and this is where it differs from the land check. The land
# check runs operator-side, uncaged, as the launcher, inside the fleet lock, so
# it executes nothing the branch wrote (0087 D6). This one runs CAGED, in a
# detached checkout of the landed commit, outside the lock, and never blocks
# (0088 D2/D3). The actor is the box's own cage tier, not the launcher, so
# running the tree's own `make` is in bounds — and it has to be, because the
# thing that went red here is a door and a door is `go test`.
#
# THE PREREQUISITES OF `make test`, DERIVED AND NOT LISTED (D6). What reds
# every seat before an arm runs is the prerequisite list of the Makefile's
# `test:` line — `tree-check`, `verify-silent-reverts` and the other doors on
# it. That list is asked of MAKE rather than pattern-matched out of the
# Makefile's text, for the reason the land check derives its arms rather than
# naming `posse_arm2 posse_arm3`: a door added tomorrow is covered by the
# commit that adds it, with nobody remembering this file. Asking make also
# reads what a seat's `make test` reads and not what a regex thinks it reads —
# prerequisites named through a variable are expanded, and a second
# `test: <door>` line elsewhere in the file is unioned in, both of which a scan
# of the first `^test:` line would miss.
#
# The probe is `-p` (print the data base) with `-n` (run nothing) over the
# tree's Makefile PLUS a second makefile holding one no-op target, which is
# then the only goal. A bogus goal would do as well on this Makefile and exit
# 2, which is a status this script could not then tell from a Makefile that
# does not parse; and on a Makefile carrying a match-anything rule it would be
# a recipe this script asked for by accident. The no-op goal exits 0 and is
# nobody's rule.
#
# That second makefile arrives on STDIN (`-f -`) rather than through a process
# substitution, and that is a cage decision rather than a style one: `<(...)`
# needs /dev/fd and a temp file needs somewhere writable, and under D3 the
# landed tree and the build cache are the only writable things there. A pipe
# needs neither.
#
# THE SUITE ARMS ARE NOT RUN. The `test:` RECIPE is about 15 minutes and three
# suite slots, per landing, competing with the seats this exists to protect
# (0088, Alternatives). Only the prerequisites run. A semantic conflict that
# only an arm reaches is residual (1) of that ADR, stated and accepted.
#
# FINDING NO PREREQUISITES AT ALL IS A RED, not a quiet skip. A derivation that
# silently returns nothing restores the exact hole this file exists to close —
# the doors vanish and the run still prints green. This repo's `test:` line has
# nine prerequisites, so a zero here is this script broken, or a Makefile that
# did not parse. The day they are genuinely retired, this reds once with that
# sentence and the fix is to delete this guard in the same commit; a check that
# could not say that is worth less than the noise.
#
# THE BODY RUNS FOUR DOORS AT A TIME (D6.7, from ranger-base-hzh0w). The
# derived goals go to ONE `make -j4 --output-sync=target`, with no `-k`.
#
# WHY, and it is a growth law rather than a speed preference: a serial
# runner's wall is the SUM of its doors, and D6 derives the body so that the
# body grows with every door anyone adds. A fixed ceiling over that sum will
# be crossed — not a risk, a consequence of the design — and D6.3 filed the
# first crossing. Under `-j` the wall follows the LONGEST door plus
# contention, so a new door adds wall only by becoming the longest, which is
# something D6's own derivation can live with. Narrowing the body was refused
# on the same grounds the derivation exists for (it gives up the derivation),
# and raising the ceiling only moves the date.
#
# 4 IS A CONSTANT HERE, not a probe of the box: `-j4` is MEASURED as the
# faster of the two readings taken, and ASSUMED to be the knee, because the
# critical path is one door (`treepins-check`) and more jobs only add
# contention to it. The readings are in ADR 0088's amendment "the body runs
# four doors at a time" and not here, for the reason the cost paragraph at
# the foot of this header gives.
#
# AND THEN THE TAIL IS MADE, IN A SECOND MAKE. The parallel run's combined
# output is captured into a shell VARIABLE and printed — a variable and not a
# file, because under D3 the landed tree and the build cache are the only
# writable things in the cage, which is the same reason the probe above is a
# pipe. On a nonzero exit the failed doors are read back out of make's own
# `*** [Makefile:N: <door>] Error` lines, and the FIRST of them is re-run
# ALONE, last: `make --no-print-directory <door>`. That run ends in the
# door's own complaint and make's line naming it, which is the tail D1 asks
# for and the same sentence a seat would read. Four properties of that, each
# of which the pin holds:
#
#   * The second make is this script, this cwd and this scrubbed environment.
#     It is not a second runner, and on a green run it never happens.
#   * A door GREEN when re-run alone is still RED here, and the last line
#     names it as such: a door not safe beside its siblings, or a flake, and
#     either one is a finding. Reading it as green would hide the one new
#     failure mode this shape creates.
#   * A red `-j` run from which no door can be parsed at all is red too, and
#     says so. Never green: a quiet green is the hole this file exists to
#     close, and it may not come back through the parser.
#   * `fmt-check` is no longer guaranteed to be the first door that can red.
#     It still reds within seconds, because it starts in the first batch.
#
# `--output-sync` needs a writable TMPDIR, and the judge is given one inside
# its own run dir (`landedRunEnv`, internal/posse/landedcheck.go). Where it is
# NOT writable, make says `cannot open output-sync lock file, suppressing
# output-sync` and carries on interleaved (MEASURED 2026-11-22, linux, GNU
# Make 4.4.1): every door still runs, and the `*** [...] Error` lines the
# re-run is chosen from are untouched, so that degradation costs legibility
# and never the verdict.
#
# THE EXIT HATCH is two deletions — `-j4 --output-sync=target` and the
# `serial_rerun` call — which restores the serial runner this replaced.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, paid outside the lock and holding nothing: see
# docs/notes.d/ranger-base-r3pqf.md, which is the ONE place this script's
# readings are said, and ADR 0088's "the body runs four doors at a time" for
# the per-door and `-j4`/`-j8` readings behind the shape above. This file
# quoting them as well would be two places to keep in step by hand, which is
# how the figure in the second one goes stale while both look authoritative
# (AGENTS.md's own rule about the tree-check reading). D6's ceiling is a 240s
# warm run, at which the price goes back to architecture (D6.2, amended by
# ranger-base-7idk4 — it was 120s, which was also the kill, and one number
# doing both jobs could only report a crossing as silence). Nothing kills on
# 240s: the kill is `landedCheckLimit`, ten minutes, and it bounds a hang.
#
# WHAT RUNS THIS: internal/posse/landedcheckplan_qa_test.go, suite arm 2, over
# a PLANTED Makefile and never over this tree. The subject is the derivation,
# and a pin that read this repo's own `test:` line would assert a door list
# every unrelated landing can move (the land check's pin says the same of its
# arms, for the same reason).
set -euo pipefail

plan_only=0
case "${1:-}" in
  '') ;;
  --plan) plan_only=1; shift ;;
  -h | --help)
    printf '%s\n' \
      'usage: posse-landed-check            judge $PWD; exit 0 green, nonzero red' \
      '       posse-landed-check --plan     print the commands it would run, run none' \
      '' \
      "ADR 0088 D1/D6. cwd is the tree to judge; it runs make test's prerequisites there."
    exit 0
    ;;
  *)
    printf 'posse-landed-check: unknown argument %s — the contract takes no arguments and judges $PWD (ADR 0088 D1)\n' "$1" >&2
    exit 2
    ;;
esac
if [ "$#" -gt 0 ]; then
  printf 'posse-landed-check: unexpected extra arguments: %s\n' "$*" >&2
  exit 2
fi

# THE CALLER'S ENVIRONMENT MAY NOT CHANGE WHAT THIS CHECK RUNS. make takes
# options out of `MAKEFLAGS` and `GNUMAKEFLAGS` and extra makefiles out of
# `MAKEFILES`, so an inherited `-n` would make every door print and run
# nothing while this exits 0 — a green over an empty room, from a variable
# nobody here typed. An inherited `-k` would carry on past the first red, and
# an inherited `-j` would change how many doors run at once: under D6.7 the
# job count and the tail are BOTH this script's to decide, and neither may be
# decided by a variable the caller exported — `-k` would put a green door's
# output after the re-run's complaint, and `-j<n>` would move the one number
# that paragraph measures. `MAKELEVEL` is the same hazard one step quieter:
# set, it makes the child announce `Entering directory` and, worse, `Leaving
# directory` LAST, so the bead gets a path where the door's name belongs.
# MEASURED 2026-11-11: this script's own pin red exactly that way when the
# suite ran it under `make test-arm2`. They are scrubbed rather than
# overridden, so the run is the same from a launcher, a seat's shell and a
# Makefile recipe.
unset MAKEFLAGS GNUMAKEFLAGS MAKELEVEL MAKEFILES MFLAGS

say() { printf 'posse-landed-check: %s\n' "$*" >&2; }
die() {
  say "$*"
  exit 1
}

# HOW MANY DOORS AT A TIME (D6.7). A constant, named once, so that the plan,
# the run and every sentence this script prints about a red quote the same
# number.
jobs_at_a_time=4

# One choke point for every child, so the plan and the run cannot disagree
# about what this check is. The step line goes out BEFORE the child, so on a
# red it is the line above the tool's complaint.
#
# It CAPTURES rather than execs through: D6.7's tail is built by reading the
# parallel run's own output back, and a narrator that handed its argv to the
# terminal could not be the thing that reads it. The transcript is reprinted
# by the caller, on stderr like everything else here, so that one stream
# carries the whole of what the runner's tail reader sees in the order it
# happened — two streams into one pipe would let a block-buffered flush land
# after a later line and scramble exactly the last three lines that reach the
# bead.
steps=0
captured=
step() {
  steps=$((steps + 1))
  say "step $steps: $*"
  captured=
  if [ "$plan_only" = 1 ]; then return 0; fi
  local rc=0
  captured=$("$@" 2>&1) || rc=$?
  return "$rc"
}

# print_captured prints the last child's transcript, and nothing at all when
# it said nothing — a blank line here is a line the runner's tail reader has
# to step over.
print_captured() {
  if [ -n "$captured" ]; then printf '%s\n' "$captured" >&2; fi
}

# The goal is named in one place, because the probe makefile and the command
# line have to agree or make builds the Makefile's default goal instead.
probe_goal=__posse_landed_check_probe__

# test_prerequisites prints the prerequisites of `test:`, one per line, in the
# order the Makefile declares them. That order no longer decides which door
# can red first — under `-j4` the first four start together (D6.7) — but it is
# still the order a seat's `make test` would name them in, and a derivation
# that reordered them would be a derivation nobody could check by eye.
#
# The data base's `# Files` section prints one `<target>: <prerequisites>`
# header per target with every variable expanded. The scan is fenced to that
# section because the `# Variables` section above it prints assignments, and a
# recipe line inside the section is TAB-indented, so neither can be read as a
# rule header. An order-only `|` separator is dropped: those are prerequisites
# too, and make takes them as goals exactly like the rest.
#
# The second colon is matched because make prints a double-colon rule as
# `test:: <prerequisites>`, and a scan that stripped one colon would hand the
# run a goal spelled `:`. That is a LOUD failure rather than a silent one —
# make refuses the goal and the landing reads red — which is precisely why it
# is worth three characters: a check that reds every landing over a Makefile
# spelling is no better than one that passes every landing.
test_prerequisites() {
  printf '%s:\n\t@:\n' "$probe_goal" |
    make -p -n -f Makefile -f - "$probe_goal" |
    awk '
      /^# Files$/ { files = 1; next }
      /^# files hash-table stats/ { files = 0 }
      /^# Finished Make data base/ { files = 0 }
      files && /^test::?([ \t]|$)/ {
        sub(/^test::?[ \t]*/, "")
        n = split($0, f, /[ \t]+/)
        for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) if (f[i] != "" && f[i] != "|") print f[i]
      }
    '
}

# failed_doors reads the doors make said died, one per line, in the order make
# said them, out of its own `*** [Makefile:N: <door>] Error <n>` lines
# (MEASURED 2026-11-22, GNU Make 4.4.1, `-j4 --output-sync=target`, two doors
# failing: one such line each, in completion order).
#
# The target is taken from after the LAST `: ` inside the brackets, because
# that is where make puts the file and line it is reporting from; a bracket
# with no `: ` at all is an older spelling (`*** [door-a] Error 1`) and the
# whole of it is the target. Nothing is filtered against the derived goals on
# purpose: the door that dies is usually a prerequisite of one of them and not
# one of them — on this repo `treepins-check` hangs off `tree-check` — and
# re-running the named door is both more precise and shorter than re-running
# its parent. A name that is not a goal at all dies at make's own `No rule to
# make target`, which is loud, and is this parser being wrong rather than a
# landing being green.
#
# `(ignored)` is excluded BELT-AND-BRACES. A recipe line prefixed `-` whose
# command fails is reported as `make: [Makefile:4: door] Error 1 (ignored)` —
# MEASURED 2026-11-22, and note it carries NO `***`, so the `***` already in
# the pattern is what excludes it today. Both clauses are kept because either
# one alone is one edit from a wrong FINDING rather than a missed one: reading
# an ignored error as the cause re-runs a door that passed, which then reports
# as "red under -j4 and green alone" on a bead. The pin holds the pair.
failed_doors() {
  awk '
    /\*\*\* \[.*\] Error/ && !/\(ignored\)/ {
      line = $0
      sub(/^[^[]*\[/, "", line)
      sub(/\].*$/, "", line)
      if (match(line, /: [^:]*$/)) line = substr(line, RSTART + 2)
      if (line != "") print line
    }
  '
}

# serial_rerun is the second make, named in ONE place so that the plan's shape
# and the red path's command cannot disagree about anything but the door.
serial_rerun() {
  step make --no-print-directory "$1"
}

say "judging $PWD"
[ -f Makefile ] || die "no Makefile here — cwd must be the root of the tree to judge (ADR 0088 D1)"

prerequisites=$(test_prerequisites)
[ -n "$prerequisites" ] || die "no prerequisites of \`make test\` found in this tree's Makefile — this repo's test: line has nine, so this is the derivation broken or a Makefile that did not parse, not a tree without doors; see this script's header"
goals=$(printf '%s' "$prerequisites" | tr '\n' ' ')
say "make test's prerequisites here: $goals"

# Unquoted on purpose: one make goal per word. They run in ONE invocation so
# that make dedupes the doors they share (fmt-check is a prerequisite of
# tree-check as well) and so that its own job server, and not a runner written
# beside it, decides what runs next. No `-k`: the run stops at the first red,
# and `--output-sync=target` keeps each door's output in one block instead of
# interleaving the four.
# `--no-print-directory` keeps the tail the door's own complaint even where
# something put make into directory-announcing mode past the scrub above.
rc=0
step make --no-print-directory "-j$jobs_at_a_time" --output-sync=target $goals || rc=$?
print_captured

if [ "$plan_only" = 1 ]; then
  say "on a red, and only then: the first failed door, alone, last — so the tail is that door's own complaint and not a green sibling's output (ADR 0088 D6.7)"
  serial_rerun '<the first failed door>'
  say "plan only: $steps steps, nothing run"
  exit 0
fi

if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
  # The parallel run's tail is a green door's output (D6.7), so the cause is
  # re-read out of the transcript rather than taken from where it landed.
  doors=$(printf '%s\n' "$captured" | failed_doors)
  first_door=${doors%%$'\n'*}
  [ -n "$first_door" ] || die "make exited $rc under -j$jobs_at_a_time and printed no \`*** [Makefile:N: <door>] Error\` line, so no door can be named or re-run — this is RED, and the cause is in the output above: make refusing a goal prints no such line (MEASURED: \`No rule to make target\`), and neither would a parser that has gone stale (ADR 0088 D6.7)"
  n_doors=$(printf '%s\n' "$doors" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
  say "red under -j$jobs_at_a_time after ${SECONDS}s: $n_doors door(s) died ($(printf '%s' "$doors" | tr '\n' ' ')) — re-running the first alone, last, so the tail is its own complaint (ADR 0088 D6.7)"
  rerun_rc=0
  serial_rerun "$first_door" || rerun_rc=$?
  print_captured
  if [ "$rerun_rc" -ne 0 ]; then exit "$rerun_rc"; fi
  # D6.7: green alone is NOT green. Either the door is unsafe beside its
  # siblings or it is a flake, and both are findings somebody has to read.
  die "$first_door red under -j$jobs_at_a_time and green alone — a door not safe beside its siblings, or a flake (ADR 0088 D6.7)"
fi

say "green in ${SECONDS}s: $(printf '%s\n' "$prerequisites" | wc -l | tr -d ' ') prerequisites of \`make test\` over $PWD, $jobs_at_a_time at a time"
